1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,750
One of the most famous cases of extinction is that of a bird known as the dodo.

2
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:11,041
In fact there's even a saying in English,

3
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:16,800
'as dead as the dodo', used to refer to something which no longer exists.

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,789
But for many centuries the dodo was alive and well,

5
00:00:20,789 --> 00:00:24,460
although it could only be found in one place

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,570
the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean.

7
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:32,180
It was a very large bird, about one metre tall

8
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:36,252
and over the centuries it had lost the ability to fly,

9
00:00:36,252 --> 00:00:40,800
but it survived happily under the trees that covered the island.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:48,940
Then in the year 1507 the first Portuguese ships stopped at the island.

11
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,495
The sailors were carrying spices back to Europe,

12
00:00:52,495 --> 00:00:58,271
and found the island a convenient stopping place where they could stock up

13
00:00:58,271 --> 00:01:05,380
with food and water for the rest of the voyage, but they didn't settle on Mauritius.

14
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:14,660
However, in 1638 the Dutch arrived and set up a colony there.

15
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:22,369
These first human inhabitants of the island found the dodo birds a convenient source of meat,

16
00:01:22,369 --> 00:01:25,260
although not everyone liked the taste.

17
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:31,760
It's hard to get an accurate description of what the dodo actually looked like.

18
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,110
We do have some written records from sailors, and a few pictures

19
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:40,440
but we don't know how reliable these are.

20
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:44,930
The best-known picture is a Dutch painting in

21
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:47,640
which the bird appears to be extremely fat,

22
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:50,370
but this may not be accurate

23
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:56,190
an Indian painting done at the same time shows a much thinner bird.

24
00:01:56,190 --> 00:02:06,340
Although attempts were made to preserve the bodies of some of the birds, no complete specimen survives.

25
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:14,321
In the early 17th century four dried parts of a bird were known to exist -

26
00:02:14,321 --> 00:02:16,150
of these, three have disappeared

27
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:24,430
so only one example of soft tissue from the dodo survives, a dodo head.

28
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:30,850
Bones have also been found, but there's only one complete skeleton in existence.

29
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:38,253
This single dodo skeleton has recently been the subject of scientific research

30
00:02:38,253 --> 00:02:46,890
which suggests that many of the earlier beliefs about dodos may have been incorrect.

31
00:02:46,890 --> 00:02:54,605
For example, early accounts of the birds mention how slow and clumsy it was,

32
00:02:54,605 --> 00:03:03,422
but scientists now believe the bird's strong knee joints would have made it capable of movement

33
00:03:03,422 --> 00:03:07,830
which was not slow, but actually quite fast.

34
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:15,830
In fact, one 17th century sailor wrote that he found the birds hard to catch.

35
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:23,340
It's true that the dodo's small wings wouldn't have allowed it to leave the ground,

36
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:28,990
but the scientists suggest that these were probably employed for balance

37
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:31,350
while going over uneven ground.

38
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:37,470
Another group of scientists carried out analysis of the dodo's skull.

39
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:47,426
They found that the reports of the lack of intelligence of the dodo were not borne out by their research,

40
00:03:47,426 --> 00:03:53,400
which suggested the bird's brain was not small, but average in size.

41
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:56,693
In fact, in relation to its body size,

42
00:03:56,693 --> 00:04:00,410
it was similar to that of the pigeon

43
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,690
which is known to be a highly intelligent bird.

44
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:11,627
The researchers also found that the structure of the bird's skull suggested that one sense

45
00:04:11,627 --> 00:04:15,860
which was particularly well-developed was that of smell.

46
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:24,774
So the dodo may also have been particularly good at locating ripe fruit

47
00:04:24,774 --> 00:04:30,260
and other food in the island's thick vegetation.

48
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:34,750
So it looks as if the dodo was better able to survive

49
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:38,610
and defend itself than was originally believed.

50
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:47,540
Yet less than 200 years after Europeans first arrived on the island, they had become extinct.

51
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,510
So what was the reason for this?

52
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:57,157
For a long time, it was believed that the dodos were hunted to extinction,

53
00:04:57,157 --> 00:05:02,380
but scientists now believe the situation was more complicated than this.

54
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:10,610
Another factor may have been the new species brought to the island by the sailors.

55
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:15,706
These included dogs, which would have been a threat to the dodos,

56
00:05:15,706 --> 00:05:22,500
and also monkeys, which ate the fruit that was the main part of the dodos' diet.

57
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,370
These were brought to the island deliberately

58
00:05:26,150 --> 00:05:30,003
but the ships also brought another type of creature -

59
00:05:30,003 --> 00:05:36,100
rats, which came to land from the ships and rapidly overran the island.

60
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:43,880
These upset the ecology of the island, not just the dodos but other species too.

61
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:49,970
However, they were a particular danger to the dodos

62
00:05:49,970 --> 00:05:51,576
because they consumed their eggs,

63
00:05:51,576 --> 00:05:57,070
and since each dodo only laid one at a time

64
00:05:57,550 --> 00:06:01,470
this probably had a devastating effect on populations.

65
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:12,990
However, we now think that probably the main cause of the birds' extinction was not the introduction of non-native species,

66
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:15,870
but the introduction of agriculture.

67
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:21,069
This meant that the forest that had once covered all the island,

68
00:06:21,069 --> 00:06:26,366
and that had provided a perfect home for the dodo,

69
00:06:26,406 --> 00:06:30,600
was cut down so that crops such as sugar could be grown

70
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:41,000
So although the dodo had survived for thousands of years, suddenly it was gone.
